  /// <summary>
  /// Language-specific text preprocessing, shipped with a language pack as a .textprep
  /// file, so that changing how a language's text is read needs a new pack rather than a
  /// new runtime. The runtime runs it on natural language text before phonemization:
  ///   1. `steps`, in order (composition, lowercasing, whitespace, replacements)
  ///   2. `number_forms`: numbers are spoken out in phonemes, using the RBNF `rule_sets`
  ///   3. `words` splits what is left into the words that are looked up and phonemized
  /// Custom pronunciation (IPA) segments are left alone - several characters these steps
  /// rewrite are IPA symbols, such as U+02C8 (primary stress) and U+0303 (nasalisation).
  /// What does not depend on language - segment splitting, audio sample request markers,
  /// speed/loudness/emotion keypoints - stays in the runtime and is not described here.
  ///
  /// The file is self-contained. Everything that depends on Unicode - which characters are
  /// letters, digits or spaces, how they lowercase, how accents compose - is data in the
  /// file, generated from Python's unicodedata when the file is built. A runtime uses no
  /// regex engine and none of its platform's Unicode functions, so every runtime reads text
  /// the same way whatever Unicode version it was built with. Text is handled as a sequence
  /// of Unicode code points throughout.
  ///
  /// major_version 3 replaced version 2's regexes and platform Unicode with the data below;
  /// version 2 had replaced version 1's fixed English number algorithm with RBNF rule sets.
  /// A runtime rejects a major version it does not know.
  /// </summary>
